## 4. Backend Architecture

### Folder Structure
```
server/
├── config/          # env loader, db connection, gemini client init
├── models/          # User.js, Scheme.js
├── routes/          # schemes.routes.js, docs.routes.js, pnl.routes.js, auth.routes.js
├── controllers/      # thin — validate input, call service, return response
├── services/         # schemeMatcher.service.js, geminiClient.service.js, pdfParser.service.js
├── middleware/       # auth.middleware.js, upload.middleware.js, errorHandler.middleware.js
└── server.js
```

### Mongoose Schema — `User.js`
```js
const userSchema = new mongoose.Schema({
  name: String,
  email: { type: String, unique: true, required: true },
  passwordHash: String,
  businessDetails: {
    businessIdea: String,
    category: String,
    isWomanFounder: { type: Boolean, default: true }
  },
  checklistProgress: {
    completeProfile:    { type: Boolean, default: false },
    findMatchingScheme: { type: Boolean, default: false },
    scanIdentityDocs:   { type: Boolean, default: false },
    generatePnLPlan:    { type: Boolean, default: false }
  },
  pnlData: Object,
  docVerification: Object
}, { timestamps: true });
```

### API Endpoint Matrix

| Method & Route | Purpose | Validations | Side Effects |
|---|---|---|---|
| `POST /api/schemes/scan` | Word-count validated idea → faiss-node vector search + Gemini embeddings → top matched schemes w/ Pros/Cons | 100–300 words, category required | Sets `findMatchingScheme = true` |
| `POST /api/schemes/generate-application` | `schemeId` + idea context → formal loan application draft | schemeId must exist in DB | none |
| `POST /api/docs/analyze` | Multipart PDF → parse → Gemini authenticity check | PDF only, ≤5MB | Sets `scanIdentityDocs = true`, saves `docVerification` |
| `POST /api/pnl/generate-pnl` | Stored idea context → 1-yr P&L metrics | Requires saved `businessIdea` | Sets `generatePnLPlan = true`, saves `pnlData` |
| `GET /api/user/checklist` | Returns live progress % + task states | Auth required | — |

Every route above returns a consistent envelope: `{ success, data, error }` — the frontend service layer should never have to branch on inconsistent response shapes.

